---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout
  title="Rock Paper Magic — LluckyGames"
  description="Five-way rock-paper-scissors with a first-to-three match structure and an AI whose patterns you can learn to read."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <header>
      <p class="faint"><a href={`${base}/projects/`}>← Projects</a></p>
      <h1>Rock Paper Magic</h1>
      <p class="muted">
        Five moves, each beats two and loses to two. First to three rounds.
      </p>
      <p><span class="pill in-progress">In progress</span></p>
    </header>

    <div class="game-frame">
      <iframe
        src={`${base}/games/rock-paper-magic/`}
        title="Rock Paper Magic — playable prototype"
        allow="fullscreen"
      ></iframe>
    </div>
    <p class="faint" style="font-size:0.82rem;margin-top:-0.75rem">
      Pick a move each round — ties replay and score nothing. First to 3 round
      wins takes the match. Try reading the Mimic, then survive the Hunter.
    </p>

    <h2>The design question</h2>
    <p>
      Rock-paper-scissors is the oldest solved game there is: pure guessing,
      no skill beyond luck. This prototype asks what the <em>minimum</em>
      change is that makes it worth playing twice. Five options instead of
      three (every move wins against two and loses to two, so the read space
      is richer), a short match structure (first to three, so one good read
      compounds across rounds instead of evaporating), and three rival AIs
      you're supposed to figure out. The throw is still luck. The match
      isn't.
    </p>

    <h2>Core loop</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Both sides pick a move <em>simultaneously</em> each round.</li>
      <li>
        Five moves — <strong>Rock</strong>, <strong>Paper</strong>,
        <strong>Scissors</strong>, <strong>Lizard</strong>,
        <strong>Spock</strong> — each beats two, loses to two.
      </li>
      <li>Win the round → one point. Tie → replay, no point.</li>
      <li>First to 3 points → <strong>WINNER</strong>. Maximum five rounds.</li>
      <li>Switch rivals mid-match: Drifter (noise), Mimic (counters your last throw), Hunter (reads your habits).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Scope (deliberately tiny)</h2>
    <p>
      No menus, no art — color, text, words. The three AI behaviors exist
      precisely so the player can <em>learn to read them</em>: that readable
      progression is the actual product. The rest is a bet on how much game
      you can get from five words and a counter table.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it lives</h2>
    <p>
      Source in
      <code>games/rock-paper-magic/</code> (Vite + TypeScript, no engine). The
      build drops into this page as an iframe — no install, no account, one
      click to play.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
